    <div class="insight-block" id="fixed-base-analysis">
      <h3>The tabletop gap extends beyond high precision</h3>
      <div class="report-prose">
        <p>
          On the three tabletop tasks labelled low or medium precision—frame placement, cup flipping
          and glasses packing—GPT-6-Astra averages 31.67% success, compared with Qwen-RobotManip’s
          91.67%. Cup flipping reaches 10% versus 90%; glasses packing reaches 20% versus 90%.
          Removing the high-precision tasks therefore does not remove the tabletop deficit.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Precision labels alone do not explain the capability gap.</strong> Depth and
          contact estimation, object orientation and coordination between the arms are plausible
          contributors, consistent with the qualitative trajectory analysis. The glasses comparison
          shows an unresolved folding and closure sequence; the frame ICL case illustrates how a
          demonstration can supply a better division of labour between the arms.
        </p>
      </div>
